use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Announcement import Announcement
from ../../../behaviors/FormParticipation import FormParticipation
from ../TextField import TextField

export ui-component TagsInput:
    description:
        A tags input is a text box that turns what the user types into separate
        tags, such as email recipients or keywords. Each tag is shown as a
        small chip in front of the text box and can be removed on its own. Its
        value is the list of tags, in the order they were added.

    composes:
        - @{TextField}

    behaviors:
        - @{FormParticipation}
        - @{Announcement}

    adding:
        Pressing Enter turns the typed text into a tag and empties the text
        box. Typing the separator, a comma by default, does the same. The
        component can choose a different separator. Spaces at the start or end
        of a tag are dropped, and empty text never becomes a tag.

    pasting:
        If the component allows it, pasted text is split at the separator and
        each piece becomes its own tag, so pasting a list of addresses adds
        them all at once.

    leaving:
        When focus leaves, the component decides what happens to text still
        in the box. It can become a tag, be cleared, or be left as it is.

    checking:
        The same tag can't be added twice unless the component allows it. The
        component can also turn a tag away by its own rule, such as not being
        an email address, and can set a limit on how many tags there are.
        Turned-away text stays in the text box so the user can fix it, and the
        component is told what was refused and why.

    removing:
        Each tag has its own button to remove it, with a name such as "Remove
        London". Pressing Backspace in an empty text box first highlights the
        last tag, and pressing it again removes that tag. The component may
        also offer a button that removes every tag at once.

    keyboard:
        When the text box is empty, or the caret is at its start, the Left
        arrow key moves onto the last tag. Left and Right then move between
        the tags, and moving Right past the last one goes back to the text
        box. Delete or Backspace removes the highlighted tag, and typing goes
        back to the text box. Whether focus moves onto the tags themselves, or
        stays in the text box while a tag is highlighted, is up to the chosen
        library.

    editing:
        If the component allows it, pressing Enter on a highlighted tag, or
        double-clicking it, turns it back into text to edit. Enter saves the
        change and Escape puts the tag back as it was. An edited tag is checked
        again like a new one.

    announcing:
        Assistive tools are told when a tag is added or removed, and which one,
        without focus moving.

    disabled:
        A disabled tags input can't be focused or changed. A read-only one
        shows its tags and can be focused, but tags can't be added, edited or
        removed.

    nonGoals:
        - No list of suggestions to pick from. Picking several values from
            given options is a separate component
        - Tags are plain text. Anything richer, such as a person with a photo,
            is up to the component showing them
